  <!-- 7. RM_Fuselight — peroxide lamp, deep floor. Hoarded fire: it farms
       the aurora's radiolysis for the biome's one native oxidizer and
       spends it in controlled cold flashes. RULED (Q5): the explosion is
       REAL, small radius, one comp — vanilla CompProperties_Explosive
       covers it outright (RM_ColdWax precedent, BlueDesert). "No yield worth
       a def" (doc, verbatim) — harvestYield left unset (0), so
       PlantProperties.Harvestable is false: there is nothing to collect,
       only a plant that can kill the pawn who tries to cut it down.

       Trigger: ordinary damage (a cutting/attack job), not a temperature
       trigger — deliberately NOT wired to Blue Desert's
       RM_CompRuinedDetonator (that class lives in a different mod's
       assembly; reusing it here would be a cross-mod C# dependency this
       build doesn't need). startWickHitPointsPercent is high so any real
       damage taken while cutting starts the wick almost immediately,
       independently matching "attempting it should read as a mistake."

       🔴 tickerType Normal, overriding PlantBaseNonEdible's inherited Long
       — TRACED, not assumed. CompProperties_Explosive.ConfigErrors demands
       Normal ("CompExplosive requires Normal ticker type"), and it is not
       cosmetic: Verse.TickManager.TickListFor dispatches a Thing into
       EXACTLY ONE of tickListNormal/Rare/Long by its own tickerType alone,
       so a Long-ticker Thing never receives a Tick() call at all — only
       TickLong() — and CompExplosive's wick countdown lives in CompTick(),
       which CompExplosive never overrides as CompTickLong(). On the
       inherited Long ticker, StartWick() would still fire (damage events
       are synchronous, not tick-driven) but wickTicksLeft would never
       decrement and Detonate() would never be called — a wick that starts
       and then simply never finishes. Accepted tradeoff, recorded per
       FOUNDRY's "specs state outcomes": Plant.TickLong() is also what runs
       growth-stage advancement and the temperature/rot dying check (Verse/
       RimWorld/Plant.cs), so Fuselight on Normal ticker never grows past
       its wildPlants spawn stage and never dies back from the universal
       -20 C ceiling the way its nine siblings do. Acceptable for a
       commonality-0.3, harvestYield-0 hazard plant whose entire point is
       the explosion, not a farmable growth cycle.

       CHILL_FIRE_BAN_1 interaction, TRACED not assumed: GenExplosion.DoExplosion
       resolves its `instigator` to the exploding Thing itself
       (CompExplosive.Detonate(): `instigator ?? parent`), and Explosion.cs's
       own fire-spawn call (`FireUtility.TryStartFireIn(c, map, size,
       instigator, ...)`) passes that same instigator through as the
       `relevantThing` RM_ChillFireGate.IsIgnitionAllowed checks via
       IsSelfOxidizing(relevantThing) — so a Fuselight explosion's own fire
       reads Fuselight itself as instigator and correctly finds the
       RM_SelfOxidizingExtension tag below, independent of which cell it
       ignites in. Verified against decompiled 1.6 source
       (Verse/GenExplosion.cs, RimWorld/CompExplosive.cs, Verse/Explosion.cs),
       not assumed from the extension's own doc comment alone. -->
  <ThingDef ParentName="PlantBaseNonEdible">
    <defName>RM_Fuselight</defName>
    <label>fuselight</label>
    <description>The only fire in the fireproof sea, and it grows on a stalk. The aurora's particle rain, working for centuries on the floor's trace ice, manufactures peroxides — the rarest substance in the biome, the only oxidizer for a thousand kilometres — and the fuselight hoards them: a dark stalk crowned with a row of small sealed ampoules, each holding a bead of oxidizer beside a bead of fuel, and a membrane between them the plant opens on purpose, one flash at a time. Every scraper is told the same thing before their first dive: the pretty one is a grenade. A fuselight brought up soaked in propane, warming, its ampoules unclenching in air, is a device; no one who harvests one does it twice.</description>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Things/Plant/RM_Fuselight/RM_Fuselight</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
